  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 156 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nBy Friday night, an upper level trough will be moving into west \nTexas, pushing the upper level ridging eastward. Southeasterly \nflow at the surface ahead of the trough will bring Gulf moisture \ninto the area as well. Lift associated with the upper trough \ncombined with increasing low level moisture, and upslope flow, \nwill combine to bring a chance for another round of showers, and \npossibly a thunderstorm or two to the entire area. However, there \nis quite a bit of spread between different models about exactly \nwhere and how much precipitation will occur, and rainfall totals \nwill vary quite a bit from around one tenth of an inch or less, up\nto around an inch. The upper level low will be slow to move out \nof the area, finally moving east into the Louisiana/Mississippi \narea by Monday evening. With mostly cloudy skies, potential \nprecipitation, and lower heights aloft, temperatures will cool \ndown into the 60s for highs over the weekend. On Monday, an even \ncooler airmass will move into the area, while we remain mostly \ncloudy, resulting in highs only making it into the 50s across the \narea. Warmer temperatures are expected to return by the second \nhalf of next week.",
